/**
 * One blueprint per resource, for every resource in the six VMware providers
 * and in the providers the Linux and Windows platforms build with (Active
 * Directory, DNS, TLS, cloud-init, Ansible, local files, random, null).
 *
 * The VMware platforms had two blueprints between them, Linux and Windows one
 * each. The providers have ~650 resources and ~15,000 arguments, and writing a
 * form for each by hand would be both a year's work and wrong within a
 * release. So the forms are made from the providers' own schemas
 * (vmware-schema-data.ts and os-schema-data.ts, generated by
 * tools/fetch-provider-schemas.mjs from `terraform providers schema -json`):
 *
 *  - every argument the resource takes is a field — required ones up top,
 *    optional ones in a collapsible section, each nested block in its own;
 *  - an optional nested block is a tick box, and its fields appear when ticked;
 *  - an argument the documentation gives a closed set for is a dropdown of it,
 *    one you can still type into, so a set the docs under-state blocks nothing;
 *  - a sensitive argument is never a text box: it is a `var.` reference, and
 *    the variable is declared `sensitive`;
 *  - a required argument left empty becomes a variable rather than a guess, so
 *    the file plans once terraform.tfvars supplies it.
 *
 * Deprecated arguments are left out — the provider accepts them only while it
 * warns — and so are computed-only ones, which nothing can set.
 *
 * Any field accepts a reference instead of a literal: `data.vsphere_datacenter.dc.id`,
 * `var.x`, `vsphere_folder.apps.path` or a function call is written unquoted.
 */

                                                                                                    
